          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Beef Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-beef-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is beef protein isolate a complete protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the product. Beef isolates made largely from collagen-rich tissue are low in leucine and tryptophan, and some brands add amino acids to round out the profile. Equip's published data, for example, shows about 0.09 g of tryptophan per serving. Check the brand's amino acid table; if none is published, assume a collagen-type profile."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is beef protein better than whey?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not for most people. Whey carries roughly two to three times the leucine per serving of the beef isolates that publish their numbers, is better studied and usually costs less. One small 2015 trial found beef isolate and whey both supported lean mass gains with training. Beef makes sense when dairy is off the table, not as an upgrade."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does beef protein powder taste like beef?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Flavored versions do not; they are sweetened and flavored like any sports shake, and buyers report that fruity flavors suit their thin texture best. Unflavored beef protein has a mild brothy note that works in soups and savory dishes but can be noticeable in a plain shake. Blending with fruit or nut butter improves body."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is beef protein isolate the same as bone broth protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They overlap. Both come from collagen-rich beef tissue and share a similar amino acid profile, and some products, such as PurePaleo, are marketed as bone broth protein isolates. Beef isolates are usually more refined and sold as sweet shakes, while bone broth powders are often savory and higher in sodium."
          }
