        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Beef Isolate Is</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#amino-profile" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Amino Acid Problem</a>
          <a href="#vs-whey" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Beef Protein vs Whey</a>
          <a href="#vs-broth-collagen" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Beef vs Bone Broth vs Collagen</a>
          <a href="#who-it-suits" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Who It Suits</a>
          <a href="#label-reading" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. How to Read the Label</a>
          <a href="#making-it-work" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Making It Work Harder</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Beef protein powder sounds like dried steak. It mostly is not. The typical beef protein isolate is made by breaking down collagen-rich beef tissue, and brands' own amino acid tables show it: glycine, proline and hydroxyproline dominate, while leucine, the amino acid that does most to switch on muscle building, runs at less than half the level found in whey. That does not make beef protein useless. It makes it a niche product for people who cannot use dairy or eggs, and one you should buy with your eyes open.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Beef Protein Isolate Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beef protein isolate is made by cooking down beef raw material, breaking the protein into smaller pieces through hydrolysis, and filtering out most of the fat and carbohydrate. The result is a powder that labels at roughly 21 to 26 g of protein per scoop with very little else. Brands describe their sources in different ways. Equip, for example, says its Prime Protein is isolated from grass-fed meat and bones and includes collagen and gelatin, while Designs for Health markets its HydroBEEF ingredient as a bone broth protein isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key word is tissue. Lean muscle meat is expensive and is sold as food. The parts left over after butchering, including bones, skin, tendons and other connective tissue, are cheap and abundant, and they are rich in collagen. Collagen is the structural protein of connective tissue, and its amino acid profile is very different from that of muscle: heavy in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, light in leucine, and missing or nearly missing tryptophan.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the brands' own amino acid tables look the way they do. Equip lists, per 25 g serving, about 5 g of glycine, 3 g of proline and 2.55 g of hydroxyproline, against about 0.93 g of leucine and 0.09 g of tryptophan. Olympian Labs, which says it adds no gelatin, collagen or single amino acids, still lists about 2.8 g of hydroxyproline and 3.2 g of proline, with about 1.1 g of leucine out of roughly 28.5 g of total amino acids. Hydroxyproline is essentially a fingerprint of collagen.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is a secret, and to their credit some brands publish the numbers. But it means beef protein isolate behaves more like a collagen supplement with some extra essential amino acids than like a scoop of ground beef. If you buy it expecting the muscle-building profile of steak or whey, the label will not deliver it. If you buy it as a dairy-free, egg-free protein and understand its limits, it can still earn a place.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Most beef protein isolate is collagen-dominant.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It is dairy-free, egg-free and nearly pure protein by the label, but leucine runs around 4 percent of the amino acids in brands that publish their profiles, against roughly 10 to 11 percent in whey. Buy it for what it avoids, not for what it builds.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, amino acid assays or taste panels for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, brands' published amino acid data, third-party testing claims and the limited research on beef protein, the method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are passing on buyer reports.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transparency counted for more here than in almost any other category. Because beef isolate's amino acid profile varies so much with its raw material, we favored brands that publish a full amino acid breakdown, and brands that say plainly whether they add collagen, gelatin or single amino acids. A beef protein that will not tell you its leucine content is asking you to trust a protein number that may not mean what you think.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then looked at protein per calorie, sweeteners, sodium and the ingredient list. Products that list anything beyond beef protein, flavor, sweetener and a thickener got a closer look, because added free-form amino acids or creatine can inflate the protein figure on a label without adding intact protein. We also checked that each product is a long-standing, widely sold option rather than a short-lived launch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we included one non-beef pick. For most people who avoid dairy but eat eggs, egg white protein is simply a more complete protein than beef isolate, and leaving it out would steer some readers to the wrong product. If you can eat eggs, read that card before you buy beef.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">21-26 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop on typical beef isolate labels</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~4%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leucine share in brands that publish profiles</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-11%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leucine share in whey protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.09 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tryptophan per serving in one brand's data</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
