        <div id="amino-profile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Amino Acid Problem: Collagen, Leucine and Added Aminos
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein quality comes down to essential amino acids, the nine your body cannot make. Muscle meat supplies all nine in generous amounts. Collagen does not: it has essentially no tryptophan and is low in leucine and several other essentials. A beef isolate made largely from connective tissue inherits much of that profile, which is why its leucine share is closer to that of a collagen supplement than to whey, egg or the beef on your plate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine is the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis after a meal. A commonly cited target is roughly 2 to 3 g of leucine per meal for adults trying to build or keep muscle. A 25 g scoop of whey delivers about 2.5 to 2.7 g. On the published numbers, a serving of beef isolate delivers about 0.9 to 1.1 g. To match whey's leucine from beef isolate alone, you would need well over twice the protein, which is a lot of powder and a lot of money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some manufacturers close the gap by adding free-form amino acids such as leucine, BCAAs or tryptophan, which can make a collagen-heavy powder technically complete. That is not inherently bad, because added leucine still signals muscle. The problem is when added aminos, glycine or creatine are used to inflate the protein figure itself, since common lab methods estimate protein from nitrogen. In Durnford v. MusclePharm, a 2018 federal appeals decision allowed a consumer to keep pursuing claims that a product labeled with hydrolyzed beef protein implied its protein came only from the named sources.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple. Read the full ingredient list. If you see beef protein isolate plus flavor and sweetener, the protein figure is mostly from beef. If you see added glycine, taurine, creatine or a list of individual amino acids, the intact protein may be lower than the headline number. Brands that publish a complete amino acid table, as Equip and Olympian Labs do, let you work out exactly what you are getting.
          </p>

